#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097467} :activity/id #uuid "8f327b60-e0cf-5553-a69d-9f75b2352ae4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049603 :topic/id #uuid "03d5463d-8db1-5e89-8946-23139052e041" :topic/math-academy-id 1100} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097467 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097828 :tutorial/content "Given two vectors, we can compute the sum by placing the head of one vector at the tail of the other.\n\nFor example, let's consider the two vectors $\\textbf{a}$ and $\\textbf{b}$ below.\n\n![](images/04/049f79ae22e5931a7ceba7f73fef5cb6bc38d17c2a45e7186d57f8760b02488a.png)\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b},$ we need to draw the second vector $\\textbf{b}$ starting from the head of the first vector $\\textbf{a}.$ Now, the sum $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ is the vector that we get when we join the tail of $\\textbf{a}$ to the head of $\\textbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/98/98a42bc3ecf7071630acc7d29349f75f77b20a9cc1c2c34382161cc79b47b4df.png)\n\nWe call this method the **triangle law of addition**. The vector obtained by adding two or more vectors is sometimes called the **resultant**." :tutorial/id #uuid "f1d80471-b824-5cac-9146-15b6e8b910d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2901 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dbd21225-959b-5fe0-a9cb-fd7a77bebb11" :step/math-academy-id 15685 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097470}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097470 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096642 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096439 :question/id #uuid "96cb0487-40a4-5942-aa43-6ca61830bde0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4272"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48176a87-796e-5637-9552-4f7fccdeee6a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050194}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510474}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding Two Vectors Using the Triangle Law"} :step/id #uuid "d2226c33-40b3-550e-9ab3-8359f4445fdd" :step/math-academy-id 15688 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097473}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096644 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096441 :question/id #uuid "6db52d38-e14d-5424-9b54-af258ad7551c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4274"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db159911-4e9a-563e-9a3d-58d4e73dc862" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572191}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding Two Opposite Vectors Using the Triangle Law"} :step/id #uuid "c5e4bcd7-3f24-565d-8d90-7d783587b66c" :step/math-academy-id 15690 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097490}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097473 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097830 :tutorial/content "Given a vector $\\overrightarrow{AB},$ the **opposite vector** is the vector $\\overrightarrow{BA}$ that has the same length but goes in the opposite direction.\n\n![](images/65/6591fd004ba2b74c2651182ce0c70302ac789d868389d057a502b8e98ac61fab.png)\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}=-\\overrightarrow{BA}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the sum of two opposite vectors,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BA},\n$$\n\nwe can use the triangle law of addition.\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ to the head of $\\overrightarrow{BA}.$ Therefore, we obtain a new vector for which the tail and head are the same point, $A.$ We denote that vector as\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BA} = \\overrightarrow{AA}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is called the **zero vector**. The zero vector has no direction and zero magnitude. With the zero vector, the head coincides with the tail." :tutorial/id #uuid "ff30d4cd-a46e-5658-aada-2885298012f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2903 :tutorial/title "Opposite Vectors and the Zero Vector"} :step/id #uuid "9589b39e-7e79-59cb-a04d-4c80d504257a" :step/math-academy-id 15691 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097475 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096646 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096443 :question/id #uuid "77d2bd5d-5f79-5204-8c65-395ef6414225" :question/math-academy-id "e-4276"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9ae0161-d016-5aef-8c11-020047c66bbd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510459}] :knowledge-point/title "Adding More Than Two Vectors Using the Triangle Law"} :step/id #uuid "79ccec21-3796-56f3-a7d4-24bb46c252eb" :step/math-academy-id 15693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097489 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096654 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096453 :question/id #uuid "5fade14b-9fa1-5777-afe3-c061907218f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4359"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "711d0e70-a248-5f93-a836-43bed5435d4f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572157}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Two Vectors Using the Triangle Law"} :step/id #uuid "9525b995-7556-5826-b108-58b033c286e1" :step/math-academy-id 16022 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097475}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097490 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097838 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to use the triangle law to add two vectors. We can also use the triangle law to subtract two vectors.\n\nThe trick is to interpret the subtraction as the addition of a negative vector. For example, the subtraction\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the addition\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{b}),\n$$\n\nwhere $-\\mathbf{b}$ is the vector that has the same magnitude as $\\mathbf{b}$ but goes in the direction *opposite* of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "9e3c330e-f67a-51e0-abd7-144acc20080b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2960 :tutorial/title "Using the Triangle Law for Subtraction"} :step/id #uuid "e1b5149f-330e-5b4d-8235-f3c9ff3b5ad7" :step/math-academy-id 16023 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097489}}] :activity/title "The Triangle Law for the Addition of Two Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096880}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572261} :activity/id #uuid "7d8c6889-f3ae-58cb-b3ca-0eb7b18e98a4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049603 :topic/id #uuid "03d5463d-8db1-5e89-8946-23139052e041" :topic/math-academy-id 1100} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572215 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64a42ea5-cb0d-54c7-b3b7-fd35361140e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/25/25b5c9f9f5f517b7a3f2f1d5e70e6d7b0183d9ab651838fe735b0f378d7f141a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571711} {:answer/id #uuid "76ed3423-8abf-5493-8cd1-018a985dcee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b5/b5a6d499f13d14643ce47f7decd46d739278c40431412c9cc1862c4dd4e61f55.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571741} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e2846a-fd9c-51da-a226-b4a0df2d034a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/26/26c4988a765a67f300428ef7e921df05bcac4f165c71f65a2ba7471d324452a8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571828} {:answer/id #uuid "af573cc2-d740-58f5-a9b3-14514a40675a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/de/de3ea7300a64b5cb83b6c06a07ed673a29a025123fc955283e13bcdcff8c0a84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571842} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a268df-28db-5d13-ab80-d2d03b65eb80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/44/44c890a2ab9f5c3ed67deecee95c76bc663010e3331da48c14d9504c242aeb2a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571894}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571842} :answer-field/id #uuid "c506eaa0-7955-591f-814f-18652414f794" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e294440f-b12e-5095-aeac-cd3f942d43e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-69856" :question/problem "![](images/32/3247a3ba0e4434860318ef697e29f7d377cfb0eb08135bb5d3e147516b2a5505.png)\n\nConsider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{CD},$ shown above. Which of the following could be $\\overset{\\to }{CD}+\\overset{\\to }{DC}?$" :question/worked-solution "According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ to the head of $\\overset{\\to }{DC}.$ Therefore, we obtain a new vector for which the tail and head is the same point, the tail of $\\overset{\\to }{CD}.$ \n\nThat vector is called the zero vector, and we write\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{CD}+\\overset{\\to }{DC}=0.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e51db7c2e9eff46679a185b82a477d65842d43d1dd71289f3e6bab1a7a503de.png)"} :step/id #uuid "28439026-9df7-5022-8efd-98cccd4a8a55"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572261 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2e99f4a-91f0-5666-986c-d96a19fd2379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/eb/eb42f18a8d83b77a18b8e796c55cb834b4288bebfd028bd89bfe58bda39252af.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095935} {:answer/id #uuid "a610a33f-18cd-5e82-8634-239c2bde6252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/24/24cf5282bb402e4c3a3320fdfbf37e6c866692e8c646d558738c289a999d3a36.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095936} {:answer/id #uuid "dee44144-6f77-53e4-9c87-f3521ff5b169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a9/a91fc49b59add56f76c2eab2def379db811cc7787ee3f809e0cb3f7c70d838df.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095937} {:answer/id #uuid "696c6af9-a8b4-5aee-9b9c-615b7c7c0075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a8/a85b7f11192f871932665f91542787d8857dcdc22f98771a8df0f4c713058e17.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095938} {:answer/id #uuid "71c2fdc3-b767-5c37-8a7c-a0da3a6be94e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/de/de4539966254b47c26b16233b26d32ad14453c296fb2692ab4bc147620c46174.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095939}] :answer-field/id #uuid "65c0f080-99fe-57f2-a719-d49f2825bb86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "91930512-85f3-5da9-9a83-797480e4b3d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-69448" :question/problem "![](images/44/44612517750f797998e4d7ec8d535cd2149c04e756255b3edf90f7a756463420.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above. Which of the following could be $-a$?"} :step/id #uuid "aacc10c9-f5e2-59c8-9018-6674e86ff34b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d17fb1-db0e-56fc-9024-2583fa21dae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f7/f7e357b3b9b74f27dc253bf611729dd188b632df511ba1f10c33e4bde313d8ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571541} {:answer/id #uuid "2cadde5b-64c5-5e69-87ce-17b7c4c4079d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/26/26bd490f6fbd3d3d9dab6a9653ed4e8043b5d266eeddd13255a30a6c2d10da0a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571614} {:answer/id #uuid "3be231ec-2961-59f0-9c62-a8218e52a1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/23/238fe7bdb6d394bed88fd0442050da14977f0391e7650ef36d7a94e998446106.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571648} {:answer/id #uuid "e103853e-2bf0-5610-8cac-ea008ed6f26f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/10/107791ab54cddf8f42b83c349d450d961f4334757f5a525e707f25382529ee02.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571942} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7107e0-b805-55a6-95df-0517737a0181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/56/56e29d1e52f2c84cf6581f9b46d4b5216f53a9c44d15b2c0c1b21d2d1f57b0b3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571648} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9df1b13-c754-54c2-9e96-913d5093b7bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9756da76-54ed-58bb-84a5-fe67fc16acc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-69508" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b6200095c8fc6c8267fcb1511fcb1ffee896c23ece3a899b76bf3a8701e65440.png)\n\nThe diagram shows the vectors $a$ and $b.$  Which of the following diagrams illustrates the vector subtraction $a-b.$" :question/worked-solution "We interpret the subtraction\n$a-b$\nas the addition\n\n$$\na+(-b),\n$$\n\nwhere $-b$ is the vector that has the same magnitude as $b$ but goes in the direction *opposite* of $b.$\n\nFinally, we use the triangle law to add the vectors $a$ and $-b:$ \n\n- We can start $a$ from an arbitrary point on the plane.\n\n- Next, we draw the vector that is equal to $-b$ and has its tail placed at the head of $a.$\n\n- Now, according to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $\\text{a}$ to the head of $-\\text{b}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/6f/6fe262898a31d393dc0e1046fbbcbb0a1c9d7c3fb00aed0a982bff5029fcd2a1.png)"} :step/id #uuid "f75a3ecc-4d92-58be-9d26-61d0b71eab9f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572215}}] :activity/title "The Triangle Law for the Addition of Two Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571527}]}
